  "profile": {
    "video_id": "kenji:SGP36xcUnOs",
    "purpose": "Demonstrates how Kenji slices, washes and marinates beef for stir-fries, and explains why each step improves texture and flavour.",
    "video_type": "how_to",
    "thesis": "Washing sliced meat in cold water before marinating, then marinating it with salt, an alkaline ingredient, cornstarch and oil, gives noticeably more tender, juicier stir-fry meat.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "Use cuts like flank, flatiron or skirt steak and slice against the grain, at a slight angle, for tenderness. Sichuan dry-fried beef is an exception where slicing with the grain gives desired chew.",
      "Washing and roughly massaging the sliced meat in cold water squeezes out excess juices so marinade penetrates better, and loosens the muscle texture. Drain and squeeze it in a fine mesh strainer afterwards.",
      "Basic marinade per pound: about 1 tsp light soy sauce, about 1/2 tsp salt, about 1 tsp Shaoxing wine or dry sherry, about 1/4 tsp baking soda (or an egg white), cornstarch, then oil added at the end.",
      "Salt dissolves some muscle protein to help retain moisture. Alkaline ingredients like baking soda or egg white tenderise. Cornstarch holds in juices. Oil keeps strips from sticking in the wok.",
      "The marinade works for beef, chicken and pork. Meat can marinate from a couple of hours up to a day, getting more tender with time.",
      "Why alkalinity tenderises meat is not fully known. One theory is that it keeps muscle proteins from bonding tightly and squeezing out juices."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "Preparing the beef",
        "summary": "Choosing cuts (flank, flatiron, skirt) and slicing against the grain at an angle, with the exception of Sichuan dry-fried beef, which is cut with the grain.",
